WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.267
Welcome to Lecture 6 of the

2
00:00:02.339 --> 00:00:05.180
FrontierCS 2 course, on bioinformatics.

3
00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:08.038
FrontierCS 2 takes a research paper

4
00:00:08.108 --> 00:00:09.850
removes its key idea from

5
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.453
the authors' own code

6
00:00:11.453 --> 00:00:14.170
and asks an AI agent to write it back.

7
00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.043
This lecture has one task: Edlib

8
00:00:17.043 --> 00:00:18.496
from a 2017 paper

9
00:00:18.572 --> 00:00:21.248
which finds the exact edit distance

10
00:00:21.325 --> 00:00:23.160
between two DNA strings.

11
00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:25.384
The task removes the recovery

12
00:00:25.448 --> 00:00:26.870
of the alignment path.

13
00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.780
With one task, we can go deeper

14
00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:30.980
in four parts.

15
00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.305
The edit distance between a query and a target

16
00:00:34.365 --> 00:00:37.050
is the smallest number of single-letter edits

17
00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:38.840
that turn one into the other.

18
00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:41.110
Each edit costs one.

19
00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.860
A substitution is a mismatch.

20
00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:46.383
An insertion is a query letter with

21
00:00:46.445 --> 00:00:47.997
no partner in the target

22
00:00:48.059 --> 00:00:50.108
and a deletion is a target letter

23
00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:51.970
with no partner in the query.

24
00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.738
Mutations and sequencing errors in

25
00:00:54.809 --> 00:00:56.800
DNA are exactly these edits.

26
00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:59.678
A table holds the distance between

27
00:00:59.746 --> 00:01:01.300
every pair of prefixes.

28
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.438
The first row and the first

29
00:01:03.505 --> 00:01:05.227
column count up from zero

30
00:01:05.227 --> 00:01:07.810
an empty string needs that many edits.

31
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.886
Every other cell takes the

32
00:01:09.952 --> 00:01:11.280
best of three moves.

33
00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:14.316
Diagonal is a match or a mismatch

34
00:01:14.316 --> 00:01:16.055
down is an insertion

35
00:01:16.055 --> 00:01:18.030
and right is a deletion.

36
00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:21.525
The answer is the bottom-right cell

37
00:01:21.525 --> 00:01:25.020
A C G T and A C C T are one edit apart.

38
00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:28.058
One step per cell means that two strings

39
00:01:28.125 --> 00:01:30.544
of thirty thousand letters need nine

40
00:01:30.612 --> 00:01:32.090
hundred million cells.

41
00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.390
The distance alone does not

42
00:01:34.454 --> 00:01:36.110
say which letters pair up.

43
00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:37.514
For an alignment

44
00:01:37.514 --> 00:01:39.869
walk back from the bottom-right cell

45
00:01:39.931 --> 00:01:41.667
each time taking a move that

46
00:01:41.728 --> 00:01:43.340
explains the cell's value.

47
00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:46.175
Edlib writes each step as a byte

48
00:01:46.175 --> 00:01:49.186
zero for a match, one for an insertion

49
00:01:49.186 --> 00:01:50.692
two for a deletion

50
00:01:50.692 --> 00:01:52.650
and three for a mismatch.

51
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.280
Here the path is zero, zero, three, zero.

52
00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:58.667
Runs of equal steps make an

53
00:01:58.743 --> 00:02:01.384
extended CIGAR string: two matches

54
00:02:01.384 --> 00:02:03.270
one mismatch, one match.

55
00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.142
Often several paths have the same cost

56
00:02:06.142 --> 00:02:08.340
and the task accepts any of them.

57
00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.464
Edlib has three modes

58
00:02:10.464 --> 00:02:12.920
which differ only in what is free.

59
00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:15.465
Global mode aligns the whole query

60
00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:16.950
with the whole target.

61
00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:18.986
Prefix mode may skip the

62
00:02:19.056 --> 00:02:20.602
target's end for free

63
00:02:20.602 --> 00:02:23.060
and infix mode may skip both ends.

64
00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:26.061
So A C T against a longer target can

65
00:02:26.135 --> 00:02:28.270
be five edits apart globally

66
00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:29.890
and zero as an infix.

67
00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:31.825
Once the ends are known

68
00:02:31.825 --> 00:02:34.468
Edlib cuts out that part of the target

69
00:02:34.468 --> 00:02:37.440
so path recovery is always a global problem.

70
00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.561
The task's DNA cores are ten thousand

71
00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:42.690
or thirty thousand bases long

72
00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:45.300
sixty to ninety-nine percent similar.

73
00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:48.450
Our own pairs have one thousand bases.

74
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.069
Pause and predict.

75
00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:52.324
At ninety-nine percent similar

76
00:02:52.324 --> 00:02:54.720
how many edits apart is our pair?

77
00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:57.110
And at sixty percent?

78
00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.086
At ninety-nine percent, seventeen.

79
00:03:03.086 --> 00:03:06.225
At sixty percent, three hundred fifty-five

80
00:03:06.225 --> 00:03:08.580
more than a third of the length.

81
00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:11.806
So: the table gives the distance

82
00:03:11.871 --> 00:03:13.628
and walking back through it

83
00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:15.123
gives an optimal path

84
00:03:15.123 --> 00:03:17.660
written as bytes or as a CIGAR string.

85
00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:20.225
The three modes differ only in which

86
00:03:20.287 --> 00:03:22.010
ends of the target are free.

87
00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.046
Next, Myers's bit-vector algorithm

88
00:03:25.046 --> 00:03:26.120
and the band.
